<section class="section" id="quickstart">
  <h2>Quickstart</h2>
  <p>Install the CLI. It needs a Go toolchain and a C compiler, because the binary links DuckDB and tree-sitter (CGO):</p>
  <pre class="code">go install github.com/pdbethke/corralai/cmd/corral@v1.0.0-rc.25</pre>
  <p>See what corral can audit in your repository. This step is free: no key, no sandbox, no model call:</p>
  <pre class="code">cd your-repo
corral certify --repo . --dry-run</pre>
  <p>
    Then audit one file in the context of the whole repository, and write the
    signed verdict to a file. <code>--repo-dir .</code> matters: without it
    corral copies only the file and its test into the sandbox, which suits a
    single self-contained file and fails on anything that imports the rest
    of your project.
  </p>
  <pre class="code">export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
corral certify --local --repo-dir . --code path/to/file.go \
  --goal "what it must guarantee" \
  --writer-model claude-sonnet-5 --mutant-model claude-sonnet-5 --critic-model claude-haiku-4-5 \
  --out record.json \
  -- go test ./...</pre>
  <p>
    The model names are an example; corral has no defaults, and you name every
    seat. Out comes a verdict, <strong>certified</strong> or <strong>needs-review</strong>, with the kill rate, the survivors, and any
    written-and-proven killing test, signed with a certify key corral keeps
    on your machine. Check the record against that key, offline:
  </p>
  <pre class="code">corral certify verify record.json --pubkey "$(corral certify pubkey)" --allow-unanchored</pre>
  <p>
    <code>verify</code> checks the record's Ed25519 signature, its hash chain,
    and that the statement is bound to that ledger head. It re-runs nothing.
    It never trusts the key embedded in the record, so it refuses to run
    without <code>--pubkey</code>; <code>--allow-unanchored</code> accepts a
    record that was not logged to a public transparency log, which a local
    run's never is.
  </p>
  <p>
    The herd runs in a sandbox: bwrap (or <code>--jail container</code> with
    docker or podman) on Linux, <code>sandbox-exec</code> on macOS. It grades
    tests in seven languages (Go, Python, Ruby, JavaScript, TypeScript, PHP
    and Rust) and writes its killing test in <em>your</em> project's harness,
    so go test, pytest, vitest, jest, minitest, RSpec, PHPUnit and cargo
    test work without configuration. The critic must be a different model
    from the writer, or the run refuses. Each seat can be a different <em>vendor</em> (a Gemini fault-planter with a Claude critic is a supported
    run), or a coding-agent CLI you already pay for (<code>claude-code</code>, <code>codex</code>) with no API key at all. <a href="/docs/language-support/">What each language supports →</a>
  </p>
  <p class="caveat">
    What the evidence behind that is: <strong>Go and Python are exercised
    hardest</strong>. Go audits run in this repository's own CI on demand
    (on pull requests labelled <code>audit</code>), and Python has had
    repeated whole-repo scans of Flask. TypeScript, JavaScript, Ruby and PHP
    each rest on a <strong>single third-party repository</strong> so far, and
    Rust on Warp's <code>fuzzy_match</code> crate; that shows the plugin works
    and is not evidence about the ecosystem. Rust has known limits: no
    coverage pre-flight, no salvage of a partly passing test, fail-fast not
    yet verified, and a source whose tests live inside it (an inline <code>mod tests &#123; … &#125;</code>) cannot be audited yet; one whose test
    module is a separate file can. <a href="https://github.com/pdbethke/corralai#readme">The evidence table is in the README →</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    Ready for more than one file? Install your project's dev dependencies first
    (the suite has to pass before corral can plant bugs against it), then audit
    the highest-ranked files with a bound on files and on money:
  </p>
  <pre class="code">corral certify --repo . --top 3 --max-tokens 3000000 \
  --writer-model claude-sonnet-5 --mutant-model claude-sonnet-5 --critic-model claude-haiku-4-5 \
  --derive-model claude-sonnet-5 \
  -- .venv/bin/python -m pytest   # the project's venv; or your own test command</pre>
  <p>
    Without <code>--top</code>, a repo scan audits up to 25 files. It runs in
    the same sandbox by default; <code>--substrate workspace</code> instead
    mutates your checkout in place, and is meant for a disposable CI runner,
    never a tree with uncommitted work.
  </p>
</section>

